(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-7-inflammatory-lung-disease-quiz"]={"title":"Inflammatory Lung Diseases Quiz 1 — CMS I Exam 7","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 7","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"Which radiographic pattern carries the best prognosis in sarcoidosis?","choices":["Hilar adenopathy alone","Parenchymal involvement alone","Adenopathy with parenchymal infiltrates","Honeycombing with hilar retraction"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with hilar adenopathy alone, stage I, have the best prognosis. Radiographic involvement of the lung parenchyma is associated with a worse prognosis, and 20% of patients with lung involvement develop irreversible lung impairment. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 45, 48"},{"q":"In which patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome does early prone positioning decrease mortality?","choices":["Cases with cardiomegaly","Patients in recovery","Moderate to severe cases","Mild cases"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Early prone positioning decreases mortality in moderate to severe acute respiratory distress syndrome, that is, a ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to inspired oxygen under 200 mm Hg. It is added to low tidal volume ventilation and treatment of the precipitant. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"Which description fits pulmonary fibrosis?","choices":["Permanent scar that stiffens the lung","Reversible swelling of the airways","Overinflated, overly compliant lung","Malignant growth in the alveoli"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Fibrosis is a response to an inflammatory source that lays down permanent scar tissue, making the lung tissue stiff and impairing gas exchange. The scarring damages the alveoli, most often permanently, and the stiff, restrictive lung causes shortness of breath. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 4, 5, 6"},{"q":"Which set lists complications of silicosis?","choices":["Multiple organ failure, refractory hypoxemia","Tuberculosis, massive fibrosis, scleroderma","Mesothelioma, lung carcinoma, pleural plaques","Uveitis, hypercalcemia, cardiac involvement"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The complications of silicosis are an increased risk of tuberculosis, progressive massive fibrosis and scleroderma. Because of the tuberculosis risk, patients should have a tuberculin skin test and a current chest radiograph. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 56, 64, 73"},{"q":"What does bronchoalveolar lavage show in silicosis?","choices":["Ferruginous bodies","Noncaseating granulomas","Thick, milky effluent","Bloody hemorrhagic fluid"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In silicosis, bronchoalveolar lavage returns a thick and milky effluent containing foamy, lipoproteinaceous macrophages. Other laboratory tests add little beyond an arterial blood gas, and pulmonary function shows reduced forced vital capacity and forced expiratory volume in 1 second. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 19, 44, 55, 62, 70"},{"q":"Besides age over 65, which finding belongs to the criteria for diagnosing idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis without biopsy?","choices":["Elevated angiotensin-converting enzyme","Bilateral hilar adenopathy","Restrictive physiology on testing","Obstructive physiology on testing"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The four criteria are idiopathic disease with inspiratory crackles on examination, restrictive physiology on pulmonary function testing, radiographic evidence of progressive fibrosis over several years, and diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing on high-resolution computed tomography. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 44, 45"},{"q":"Below what ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen is the oxygenation criterion for acute respiratory distress syndrome met?","choices":["100 mm Hg","200 mm Hg","300 mm Hg","400 mm Hg"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. The oxygenation impairment threshold is a ratio of arterial oxygen pressure to fraction of inspired oxygen under 300 mm Hg (or a ratio of arterial oxygen saturation to fraction of inspired oxygen under 315). Within it, 200 to 300 is mild, 100 to 200 moderate and under 100 severe. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"Which imaging study is preferred for asbestosis?","choices":["Computed tomography","Plain chest radiograph","Echocardiography","Pleural fluid cytology"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography is the preferred imaging for asbestosis. It can show pleural plaques, alveolitis and fibrosis, with honeycombing in advanced disease, while the plain chest radiograph is often normal, especially early in the disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 58, 59, 73"},{"q":"Which cause of acute respiratory distress syndrome is a direct pulmonary insult?","choices":["Sepsis","Severe pancreatitis","Multiple blood transfusions","Aspiration of gastric contents"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Direct pulmonary causes include aspiration of gastric contents, diffuse pneumonia, lung contusion, toxic gas inhalation, near-drowning, and fat, air, amniotic fluid or thrombus emboli. Sepsis, severe pancreatitis, nonthoracic trauma, drug overdoses and multiple blood transfusions are systemic causes. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 72"},{"q":"Over what time course do most interstitial lung diseases present?","choices":["Months to years","Days to weeks","Weeks to months","In discrete episodes"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Most interstitial lung diseases present chronically, over months to years; idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis, sarcoidosis, pneumoconiosis and connective tissue diseases present this way. Acute, subacute and episodic presentations are the less usual patterns. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"Which part of the lung does interstitial lung disease involve?","choices":["The pleural lining","The large conducting airways","The pulmonary arteries","The gas-exchanging parenchyma"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease involves the parenchyma, the portion of the lung involved in gas transfer: the alveoli, alveolar ducts and respiratory bronchioles. It is characterized by alveolar septal thickening, fibroblast proliferation, collagen deposition and, if prolonged, pulmonary fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 3"},{"q":"When may the physical examination be normal in interstitial lung disease?","choices":["With advanced fibrosis","Once dyspnea occurs at rest","With right heart failure","Early in its course"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Early in interstitial lung disease the examination may be normal. As the disease advances, small lung volumes, bibasilar dry rales or inspiratory crackles and distal clubbing appear, and right heart failure can develop with advanced disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 23, 24"},{"q":"Which finding gives a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis?","choices":["Serum antibodies to the agent","White cell count with left shift","Restrictive pulmonary function","Little change on acute imaging"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Antibodies to the agent in serum give a definitive diagnosis of hypersensitivity pneumonitis. Supporting findings include an elevated white blood cell count with a left shift, hypoxia and restrictive pulmonary function, and lung biopsy may be indicated in subacute or chronic disease. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 57, 69"},{"q":"How does cigarette smoking change the risks of asbestos exposure?","choices":["It leaves the radiograph unchanged","It speeds clearance of the fibers","It sharply raises lung cancer risk","It lowers the risk of mesothelioma"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In asbestos exposure, cigarette smoking increases the prevalence of radiographic pleural and parenchymal changes and markedly increases the incidence of lung carcinoma; the increased risk of lung cancer and mesothelioma applies especially to smokers. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 55, 56"},{"q":"Which measure belongs to the supportive care of an adult with coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":["Influenza and pneumococcal vaccines","Lavage to remove the coal dust","Routine pulmonary artery catheter","Higher hemoglobin targets"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Supportive care for coal workers' pneumoconiosis includes oxygen, pneumococcal and influenza vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, because no effective curative treatment is available. Lung transplant may be indicated in severe cases. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 54, 56, 74"},{"q":"Which computed tomography finding characterizes idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia?","choices":["Upper-lobe nodular opacities","Patchy pleural-based honeycombing","Eggshell hilar node calcification","Pleural plaques at the bases"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Computed tomography in idiopathic fibrosing interstitial pneumonia shows diffuse patchy fibrosis with pleural-based honeycombing, while the chest radiograph shows progressive fibrosis over several areas. On high-resolution scanning in a patient over 65, it is one of the four no-biopsy criteria. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 34, 35, 53, 58, 60, 63"},{"q":"For which idiopathic interstitial pneumonia subtype may corticosteroids be used?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Acute interstitial pneumonia","Desquamative interstitial pneumonia","Cryptogenic organizing pneumonia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Corticosteroids may be used in some scenarios of idiopathic interstitial pneumonia: cryptogenic organizing pneumonia, lymphoid interstitial pneumonia and nonspecific interstitial pneumonia. Symptoms vary with the subtype, and the treatment plan depends on the subtype diagnosed. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 31, 37, 38"},{"q":"How can mesothelioma frequently be diagnosed?","choices":["Pleural fluid cytology","Serum angiotensin-converting enzyme","Tuberculin skin test","Serum antibodies to the agent"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Patients with mesothelioma frequently develop pleural effusions, and the diagnosis can be made from pleural fluid cytology. Mesothelioma is primarily a pleural cancer, nearly always caused by asbestos exposure, with a 10% lifetime risk in asbestos workers. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 44, 59, 64, 69"},{"q":"At what hemoglobin level is transfusion indicated in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["5 g/dL","7 g/dL","9 g/dL","10 g/dL"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Patients with acute respiratory distress syndrome are transfused at a hemoglobin of 7 g/dL; higher hemoglobin targets do not improve patient outcomes. Supranormal, inotrope-driven oxygen delivery is avoided because it can cause harm. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph finding points to sarcoidosis or silicosis among the interstitial lung diseases?","choices":["Pleural-based honeycombing","Hilar and mediastinal adenopathy","Pleural plaques at the bases","Normal heart, air bronchograms"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. On chest radiography, hilar and mediastinal lymphadenopathy points to sarcoidosis or silicosis. Sarcoidosis shows symmetric bilateral hilar and right paratracheal adenopathy, and silicosis can calcify the periphery of the hilar nodes in an eggshell pattern. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 34, 44, 58, 63, 73"},{"q":"Which drug is both an immunosuppressant used in sarcoidosis and a listed cause of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Azathioprine","Infliximab","Amiodarone","Methotrexate"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Methotrexate is one of the immunosuppressants most commonly used when sarcoidosis is corticosteroid-intolerant or refractory, and it treats many autoimmune diseases such as lupus and rheumatoid arthritis. It is also implicated as a cause of interstitial lung disease in the drug list and the treatment-related box. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 12, 18, 46, 72"},{"q":"Within how many days are corticosteroids conditionally supported by guidelines in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["2 days","7 days","14 days","30 days"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Guidelines conditionally support corticosteroids in acute respiratory distress syndrome when given within 14 days. Other pharmacologic measures reduce oxygen demand with sedatives, analgesics and antipyretics, while neuromuscular blockade remains controversial. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 73, 74"},{"q":"In interstitial lung disease, what does a positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) test suggest?","choices":["Active sarcoidosis","Granulomatosis with polyangiitis","Goodpasture syndrome","Collagen vascular disease"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A positive c-ANCA (cytoplasmic antineutrophil cytoplasmic antibody) points to granulomatosis with polyangiitis, formerly Wegener's, a granulomatous vasculitis that is one of the interstitial lung diseases with a granulomatous response. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 21, 25, 44"},{"q":"Which statement fits the definition of interstitial lung disease?","choices":["Neither malignant nor infectious","A single disorder with one cause","An infection of the alveoli","A cancer of the lung tissue"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Interstitial lung disease is a broad category of more than 200 disorders characterized by scarring (fibrosis) and/or inflammation of the lung parenchyma. By definition it is not malignant and not caused by an infectious etiology. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 3, 56, 59"},{"q":"When may lung transplant be indicated in coal workers' pneumoconiosis?","choices":["In simple disease","At the time of diagnosis","When pleural plaques appear","In severe cases"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Coal workers' pneumoconiosis has no effective curative treatment, so care is supportive with oxygen, vaccinations, rehabilitation and smoking cessation, and lung transplant may be indicated in severe cases. Complicated disease can progress to progressive massive fibrosis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 51, 54, 58"},{"q":"Which disorder results from an organic rather than an inorganic occupational or environmental exposure?","choices":["Coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Silicosis","Asbestosis","Bird fancier's lung"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. The organic exposures that cause interstitial lung disease include bird fancier's lung, farmer's lung, mushroom worker's lung and hot tub lung. Inhaled organic dusts such as bird excreta and feathers cause hypersensitivity pneumonitis. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 9, 13, 65"},{"q":"Which interstitial lung disease can produce an episodic presentation?","choices":["Idiopathic pulmonary fibrosis","Coal workers' pneumoconiosis","Drug-induced lung disease","Hypersensitivity pneumonitis"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. An episodic presentation is less common in interstitial lung disease and can be seen with hypersensitivity pneumonitis, where inhaled organic material leads to illness after exposure, and symptoms can be reversible if the problem is caught early. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 22, 65"},{"q":"Which chest radiograph stage of sarcoidosis shows hilar adenopathy together with parenchymal involvement?","choices":["Stage I","Stage II","Stage III","Stage IV"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Stage II sarcoidosis shows hilar adenopathy with parenchymal involvement. Stage I is bilateral hilar adenopathy alone, stage III is parenchymal involvement alone with diffuse reticular infiltrates, and stage IV is advanced fibrosis with honeycombing. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 45"},{"q":"Which fluid strategy is recommended in acute respiratory distress syndrome?","choices":["Inotrope-driven supranormal delivery","Routine catheter-guided fluids","Conservative, restrictive fluids","Liberal fluid resuscitation"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A conservative fluid protocol targets a central venous pressure under 4 mm Hg; restrictive fluids significantly increase ventilator-free days and shorten intensive care unit stays. Routine pulmonary artery catheters and supranormal oxygen delivery targets are avoided. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slide 74"},{"q":"In which decades of life does sarcoidosis usually begin?","choices":["First and second","Third and fourth","Fifth and sixth","Seventh and eighth"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Sarcoidosis has its onset in the third and fourth decades of life. It more frequently affects women and has a higher occurrence in North American Black and Northern European white populations; its etiology is unknown. — Inflammatory Lung Disease 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 40"}]};
